From c94003871bac649beea7be3fcc121c4108cec330 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:56:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 35/91] P1 review fixes, and the drop rule next to runahead -
 pins: a context that builds graphs on the head itself (MTP of the same model,
 a DFlash / EAGLE3 draft through ctx_other) pins it for its lifetime, P1
 neither releases a pinned head nor runs next to MTP hidden states switched on
 at run time; every context starts with the head on the card, and one freed in
 the middle of a prompt gives it back; a failed read keeps a (head-less)
 scheduler instead of none, and the destructor tolerates none; a start-up
 probe reserves the -ub buffers with the head out, so a -ub too large even
 then fails at load (P1 off) instead of in the middle of a request; reserve
 failures in the P1 transitions fail the call, not the process; a call that
 failed in its ubatch loop no longer leaves its output groups behind; P1 is
 off for contexts with several sequences (--parallel), where a prompt chunk
 next to another sequence's token would move the head on every call;
 ggml_backend_cuda_trim synchronizes every device before trimming its pools.
 Drop rule + runahead (LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_DROP_W with an explicit
 LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_RUNAHEAD): the first half takes the routing weights and
 applies the rule, the exact path's multipliers live in exact_mask
 (llama_moe_stream_exact_mask), the second half maps dropped pairs onto a kept
 slot of their token; the first half no longer looks up the slot of a dropped
 expert (std::out_of_range in a worker, a silent crash). SiegeLadder runs.md
 4.51: P1 bitwise again (129 of 129 rows); drop 0.14 + runahead KL 0.0097 vs
 0.0150 alone, but no faster than runahead (runahead gives the experts slots,
 so the rule fires 7x less)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
